1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back to the VentureX tutorial series.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,500
This video is about the feature that
sets VentureX apart: its AI engine.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,500
VentureX doesn't just connect to
one AI provider — it intelligently

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,000
routes tasks across multiple providers
based on what you're trying to do.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,500
Let me show you how it works.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,500
The AI Dashboard is your command center
for everything AI in VentureX.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,500
At the top, you see your current plan
and usage — how many API calls

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,000
you've made this billing cycle,
your remaining quota, and your cost breakdown.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,000
Below that, the Provider Status panel
shows which AI providers are connected

10
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:37,000
and their health. On the right,
recent AI queries —

11
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,500
every request your system has made,
which provider handled it, and the token count.

12
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,500
Let's break down each section.

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,500
Navigate to Usage & Plan.
Your plan determines your monthly quota.

14
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,000
The Free tier gives you 1,000 AI requests
per month. Pro gives you 10,000.

15
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,500
Enterprise is unlimited with
dedicated infrastructure.

16
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,500
Each request has a token cost — and
tokens are how AI providers bill you.

17
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:02,000
A typical search query might use 200 tokens,
while a complex analysis could use 2,000.

18
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,500
VentureX tracks all of this in real-time.

19
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,500
The usage breakdown shows you which
features are consuming AI the most —

20
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,000
search, insights, recommendations,
and document analysis.

21
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,500
This helps you understand where your
AI budget is going and optimize accordingly.

22
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,500
Here's where VentureX gets really smart.
The multi-provider architecture lets you

23
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:24,000
configure multiple AI services and
route tasks to the best one for the job.

24
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:27,000
Let's look at the providers page.

25
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,000
You'll see up to three provider cards:
Google Gemini, NVIDIA NIM, and OpenAI.

26
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,500
Each shows connection status,
API key configuration, and current health.

27
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,500
Let me explain why three providers.

28
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,500
VentureX uses intelligent task routing.
When you perform an AI action,

29
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,000
the system analyzes the task type
and sends it to the optimal provider.

30
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:49,000
Creative tasks like generating marketing
copy, brainstorming, or content creation —

31
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:52,500
those go to Gemini, which excels at
creative and nuanced language.

32
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,500
Code-related tasks — generating SQL queries,
writing code, debugging —

33
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:00,000
those route to NVIDIA's NIM models,
which are optimized for code generation.

34
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,000
General tasks — summarization, Q&A,
data extraction, standard analysis —

35
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,500
those go to OpenAI, which has the
broadest general capability.

36
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,000
The routing is automatic.
You don't have to think about it.

37
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,500
But if you want to override it,
you can pin a specific provider

38
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:17,500
for specific features in Settings.

39
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,500
Let me show you how to configure
a provider. I'll set up NVIDIA —

40
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,000
it has a generous free tier.
First, go to build.nvidia.com

41
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:28,500
and create a free account.
Once you're in, navigate to your API keys

42
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:32,000
section and generate a new key.
Copy that key — it starts with 'nvapi-'.

43
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:35,500
Back in VentureX, paste it into the
NVIDIA API key field and click Save.

44
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:39,500
VentureX immediately tests the connection.
If the key is valid, you'll see

45
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:43,000
the green Connected status.
The free tier gives you a generous

46
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,500
monthly allowance — enough for most
small to medium businesses.

47
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:50,000
If you hit the limit, tasks automatically
fail over to the next available provider.

48
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,000
With all three providers connected,
you have maximum redundancy

49
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:57,500
and the best task routing.
Let me show you the health monitoring.

50
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:01,500
The AI Health page gives you
real-time visibility into provider performance.

51
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:05,000
Response time — how fast each provider
is responding to requests.

52
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:08,500
Error rate — percentage of failed requests.
Uptime — availability over the last 30 days.

53
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:12,000
Rate limit status — how close you are
to hitting provider-imposed limits.

54
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,500
If a provider goes down or slows
significantly, VentureX automatically

55
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,000
routes around it. You'll see an alert
here and in the notification center.

56
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:23,000
The quota system lets you set limits
per user and per company —

57
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:26,500
if you're a multi-tenant installation.
You can allocate monthly request limits,

58
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:30,000
set token budgets, and even restrict
which providers specific users can access.

59
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:34,000
For example, your sales team gets
500 requests on OpenAI for general analysis,

60
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:37,500
while your dev team gets 1,000 requests
routed through NVIDIA for code tasks.

61
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,000
This granular control prevents runaway
costs and ensures fair usage

62
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:44,000
across your organization.

63
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:48,000
Rate limiting protects both your budget
and your provider relationships.

64
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:51,500
You can set per-minute, per-hour, and
per-day limits for each provider.

65
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,000
Burst limits handle sudden spikes —
like when multiple users run reports

66
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,000
simultaneously. Cool-down periods
prevent abuse.

67
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,500
And priority queues ensure that
critical operations — like a sales rep's

68
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:05,000
deal analysis — aren't blocked by
background batch processing.

69
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:09,000
Let me show you AI in action.
We're in the CRM module with Sarah Mitchell's

70
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,000
contact. See the AI sidebar
or floating button?

71
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:15,000
Let's use AI-powered search.

72
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:19,000
AI Search lets you ask questions in
natural language instead of building

73
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,500
complex filters. Watch:
'Show me all contacts at technology companies

74
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:26,000
with deals over $10,000 in the
negotiation stage.'

75
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:29,500
VentureX parses this, routes it to
the appropriate provider,

76
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,500
and returns structured results.

77
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,500
AI Insights analyze a record and
surface patterns. On this deal, it's

78
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:40,000
telling me: 'This deal has been in
Negotiation for 14 days —

79
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,500
average for this deal size is 7 days.
Consider scheduling a follow-up.'

80
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:47,000
It's also flagging: 'Sarah Mitchell has
been highly engaged — 12 activities in

81
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,000
30 days. Strong relationship indicator.'

82
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:54,000
AI Recommendations in the Sales Pipeline
suggest next actions for each deal.

83
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,500
It might recommend moving a stale deal
to a different stage,

84
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:01,000
flagging a high-value deal that needs
attention, or suggesting cross-sell

85
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:04,000
opportunities based on deal history.

86
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:08,000
Let me show you the fallback system
in action. If I temporarily disable

87
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:11,500
one provider — say OpenAI — and run
a general query, watch what happens.

88
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:15,000
The system detects OpenAI is unavailable,
checks the task type, and routes it

89
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:18,500
to the next available provider —
Gemini in this case.

90
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:22,000
You can see in the Recent Queries log:
'Provider: Gemini (fallback from OpenAI).'

91
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:25,500
Your users never see an error —
the system self-heals.

92
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:29,000
You can configure the fallback order
in Settings, and you'll get an alert

93
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:32,500
whenever a fallback is triggered
so you know a provider needs attention.

94
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:36,500
That's the AI engine in VentureX —
multi-provider routing,

95
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:40,000
intelligent task allocation,
health monitoring, quota management,

96
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:43,500
and automatic fallback.
It's AI that works for you,

97
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,500
not the other way around.

98
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:50,000
In the next video, we'll cover
Accounting and Financial Management.

99
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,500
Like and subscribe, and I'll see you
in the next one.
